module Formatter.Audit.PredicateAgreement exposing
    ( Finding
    , auditLpt
    , findingsToJson
    )

{-| A render-truth audit of the "will this break?" predicates in
`Formatter.Render.NodeClassify`.

This module backs the CLI's `--audit-predicates` flag:

    gren-format --audit-predicates MyFile.gren

which walks a file's LPT and prints, as JSON, every predicate that promised a
break the renderer did not deliver. Like `Formatter.Audit.DecisionTrace` it sits
off to the side of the pipeline; nothing in `Formatter.Logical` or
`Formatter.Render` consults it. `tests/audit-predicates.py` is the gate that
drives it over the fixture corpus, and `docs/testing.md` describes how to run
that and how to read what it prints.


## Why it exists

Layout here is decided in two stages. A predicate in `NodeClassify` answers
"does this subtree force a hard break?" *before* anything is rendered, so its
callers can lay out the code around it. Any such predicate is a hand-written
mirror of what the renderer will do, and nothing forces the two to agree. When
one over-approximates -- claims a break the renderer never emits -- callers
commit to a vertical shape for content that lands on one line.

Nothing else in the repo catches that. Such output is still deterministic,
AST-equivalent, idempotent, and stable under both fuzzers; only the layout is
wrong. This module is the missing oracle: it checks each predicate against the
renderer itself.


## The property

For the predicates in `auditedPredicates` the implication is one-directional:

    predicate node == True   ==>   the node's own box renders multi-line

An under-approximation -- `False` on a node that does render multi-line -- is
deliberately not reported. These predicates claim only the breaks that are
unconditional, and a node can still break for reasons they do not model, most
often the author's own row layout (`forceVertical`).

`commentBreaksFlowRow` is audited separately and in **both** directions, because
it is a different kind of mirror and its dangerous direction is the other one.
See `flowCommentFindings`.


## Why the one-directional half is nearly empty

That is the intended end state rather than missing coverage. This module was
written when `NodeClassify` carried a whole mirror layer --
`subtreeHasVerticalBox`, `nodeSpansRows`, `bracketOpenGate` and friends, each
predicting verticality from source rows. All of them are gone: verticality is
now decided by rendering the child and asking `isSingleLine` / `B.allSingles`,
which cannot disagree with the renderer because it *is* the renderer. What
remains is `isMultilineLambdaParenBlockBox`, a structural query over shape kinds
rather than a prediction from rows -- so that half of the audit is close to
vacuous by construction, and shrinking it to that was the goal.

@docs Finding, auditLpt, findingsToJson

-}

import Formatter.Logical.LogicalPrintingTree exposing
    ( LPNode
    , LPShape(..)
    , lpnChildren
    , lpnFirstPos
    , lpnShape
    , stypeToString
    )
import Formatter.Render.Box as B exposing (Box)
import Formatter.Render.BoxOps exposing (isSingleLine)
import Formatter.Render.FlowAssembly exposing (FlowMeasure)
import Formatter.Render.MakeRenderBox exposing
    ( buildFlowBoxMeasured
    , makePBox
    )
import Formatter.Render.NodeClassify exposing
    ( commentBreaksFlowRow
    , isMultilineLambdaParenBlockBox
    , nodeIsComment
    )
import Json.Encode as Json


{-| One disagreement: `predicate` claimed the `boxKind` node at `row`:`col`
would break, but it rendered as the single line `rendered`.

`propagated` distinguishes a wrong answer from its echo. A predicate whose
fallback arm is `Array.any thePredicate children` -- as the retired
`subtreeHasVerticalBox`'s was -- turns one wrong answer at a leaf into a wrong
answer at every ancestor above it, and at every caller reading those. A
propagated finding is real but not separately fixable: it disappears once the
node below it is fixed. Only `propagated == False` findings are a work-list.

Neither predicate audited today is recursive (see the module doc), so nothing
they report can be an echo and this flag should always be `False`. It is kept
because it is the shape any future recursive predicate would need, and because a
`True` here would itself be worth investigating.

`claim` is what the predicate answered. Findings of the one-directional property
all carry `claim == True` by construction; `flowCommentFindings` also reports
`False`, and the two read as opposite complaints.

`boxKind` is misnamed: it holds `shapeKindName (lpnShape node)`, an `LPShape`
constructor name, not a `Formatter.Render.Box` -- the two are different things
(a shape says what kind of node this is, a `Box` says where its characters
land), and the name dates from before `LPBox` was renamed to `LPShape`.
`shapeKind` is what it should be called. It is deliberately NOT renamed: the
field is a JSON key in the `--audit-predicates` output, read by
`audit-predicates.py`, `matrix-syntax.py`, `gen-random.py` and the two
`_run_predicate_*` instruments, and named in `tests/GENERATOR.md` and
`docs/testing.md`. Renaming it is a one-shot flag day across all of those, for
a field whose only consumers are our own gates -- not worth breaking a saved
finding or a half-updated instrument over. Read it as "shape kind".
-}
type alias Finding =
    { predicate : String
    , boxKind : String
    , row : Int
    , col : Int
    , rendered : String
    , propagated : Bool
    , claim : Bool
    }


{-| The predicates checked against the one-directional property. Each is
consulted before layout and answers "this will break", so each owes the renderer
agreement.

Adding a predicate here is the cheap half of adding one to `NodeClassify`. The
expensive half is that a new shape predicate read from source rows is a step
back toward the mirror layer this codebase spent a long refactor deleting -- the
one `tests/check-render-invariant.py` now enforces the absence of. Prefer
rendering the child and observing its box.
-}
auditedPredicates : Array { name : String, test : LPNode -> Bool }
auditedPredicates =
    [ { name = "isMultilineLambdaParenBlockBox", test = isMultilineLambdaParenBlockBox } ]


{-| Walk every node of an LPT and report each predicate that promised a break
the renderer did not deliver.
-}
auditLpt : LPNode -> Array Finding
auditLpt root =
    (walk root).findings


{-| Bottom-up so each node knows whether the lie started here or below it.
`liesBelow` is threaded up rather than recomputed per node, which is what makes
the walk linear: a recursive predicate reads its descendants, so a descendant
that lies explains an ancestor that lies, and re-deriving that per node would
re-walk each subtree once per ancestor.
-}
walk : LPNode -> { findings : Array Finding, liesInSubtree : Bool }
walk node =
    let
        childResults =
            Array.map walk (lpnChildren node)

        liesBelow =
            Array.any (\r -> r.liesInSubtree) childResults

        ownFindings =
            checkNode node liesBelow
    in
    { findings = Array.mapAndFlatten (\r -> r.findings) childResults ++ ownFindings
    , liesInSubtree = liesBelow || not (Array.isEmpty ownFindings)
    }


{-| The `commentBreaksFlowRow` audit: does the predicate still agree with
`FlowPolicy.decide` about which comments stop a following item from sharing
their row?

That predicate is a different animal from the ones in `auditedPredicates`. It is
not a shape prediction about one subtree; it is a hand-written summary of
`decide`'s separator table -- "a `--` always breaks, a multi-line `{- … -}`
always breaks, a single-line one MID-FLOW does not, and only when a real item
follows" -- and its own docstring says in so many words that it **must track
`decide`**. Nothing enforced that, and `decide` has grown a separator since it
was written.

It also fails the other way round from the shape predicates, so this audit is
**bidirectional**. Over-approximating makes a caller break a flow that renders on
one line. Under-approximating is the original bug this predicate was added to
fix, and it is the worse one: `forceVertical` comes from AST rows that contain no
comments, so the first format renders flat, the comment breaks the row anyway,
and the reparse -- now seeing a later sibling on a later row -- renders broken.
The file oscillates.


### The grain is the comment RUN

The check is per maximal run of comments in one gap -- not per flow, and not per
comment -- because that is the grain the claim is made at: assemble the children,
then for each run ask whether the next real item started a row of its own, and
compare that against `commentBreaksFlowRow` applied to that run plus the item
after it. Answering from `decide`'s own assembly, rather than from a second
prediction, is the whole point; every other reason a flow breaks moves both sides
and cancels.

Asking it per COMMENT instead was wrong, and expensively so: it reported
thousands of `--comment-runs` matrix cells and not one of them was a layout bug.
Removing one member of a run does not close the gap, because the other member
breaks the row anyway, so a `--` in a run read as an over-claim; and removing a
single-line `{- b -}` that merely OCCUPIES a row a `--` before it created does
close one, so it read as an under-claim. Both directions are the same mistake:
the audit measured a member's own contribution while the predicate claims what
the *gap* does, and those coincide only when the member is the sole reason for
the break. In a run there is always another reason. A run of one is the
single-comment case unchanged.


### Only a run with a real item on BOTH sides is checked

Three exclusions, and the scope is load-bearing. The first two are the predicate
being deliberately narrower than `decide` rather than wrong; the third is this
measurement having nothing to say:

  - **A trailing comment** has nothing after it to push. `foo bar -- c` is one
    line and is the long-standing fixed point for that shape; the predicate says
    so in as many words.

  - **A LEADING comment** is where the two genuinely disagree. `decide`'s
    `BlockCommentItem` arm breaks a *standalone leading* block comment in a
    vertical context, so `{- c -}` / `42` is what it assembles, while the
    predicate's rule is scoped to mid-flow and answers False. Nothing is misled
    by that, and the argument is worth keeping because it is one nobody had
    written down: the predicate exists to stop the *items* being re-read as
    author-broken on the reparse, and rows a leading comment occupies above the
    whole construct are attributed to the comment, not to a break between items.
    The flow also breaks by itself there, so the caller's `else` branch produces
    the same layout the `True` branch would have been reached for. Should either
    of those stop holding, this exclusion is the thing to revisit first.

  - **A gap the two items do not share even without the run** (`gapWithout > 0`).
    There the difference of the two gaps measures how many EXTRA rows the run
    added, and the predicate does not claim that; it claims the following item
    starts a fresh row, which is already true with the run deleted. Nothing is
    misled either, because the caller ORs this predicate into a `forceVertical`
    that the already-broken gap sets anyway -- the two arms agree, so the
    predicate's answer cannot reach the output. The dangerous direction is
    untouched: an under-claim only oscillates when the flow WOULD have been flat
    there, which is `gapWithout == 0`. A `{- ¤1 -} -- ¤2` in front of a record
    type that takes the `DropBlock` rule on its own account is the shape this
    excludes.

    It replaced an earlier fourth exclusion that it subsumes.
    `runPairsWithFollower` asked `FlowAssembly.pairLeadingComments` whether the
    assembler attaches the comment to the item after it -- the case being that
    the item's own placement moved it (a type record's `DropBlock`, a soft block
    that rendered multi-line) and the comment only travelled along. That is the
    same situation seen through a prediction: an item that moves on its own
    account is an item the gap stays open without, so `gapWithout > 0` states it
    in terms of the assembly instead. It also covers what the pairing missed --
    `pairLeadingComments` walks past *pairable* comments only, so a `--` in the
    run breaks the chain and nothing pairs. It was deleted after measuring, not
    by argument: disabled, it changed nothing over the corpus or either matrix
    axis.

Scoped to the shape kinds whose children `makePBox` hands straight to the gluing
flow assembler, so the assembly audited is the one the renderer performs. NOT
covered: `BinopLayout.commentBreaksBinopChain`, which asks the same predicate of
a chain with its operators interleaved back in -- reconstructing that here would
be a third copy of the interleaving, which is the kind of thing this module
exists to object to.
-}
flowCommentFindings : LPNode -> Bool -> Array Finding
flowCommentFindings node liesBelow =
    let
        children =
            lpnChildren node
    in
    if not (dispatchesToGluingFlow (lpnShape node)) || not (Array.any nodeIsComment children) then
        []

    else
        commentRuns children
            |> Array.map (\run -> runFinding node liesBelow children run)
            |> Array.mapAndKeepJust identity


{-| The maximal runs of consecutive comment children, as `{ start, end }` with
`end` exclusive — one entry per GAP that holds comments, which is the grain the
claim is made at. -}
commentRuns : Array LPNode -> Array { start : Int, end : Int }
commentRuns children =
    children
        |> Array.indexedMap
            (\i _ ->
                if isCommentAt i children && not (isCommentAt (i - 1) children) then
                    Just { start = i, end = i + commentPrefixLength (Array.dropFirst i children) }

                else
                    Nothing
            )
        |> Array.mapAndKeepJust identity


{-| Is the child at `i` a comment? False when `i` is out of range. NB Gren's
`Array.get -1` wraps to the LAST element, so index -1 must not reach it: a flow
whose last child is a comment would otherwise report its first child as mid-run
and lose that run. -}
isCommentAt : Int -> Array LPNode -> Bool
isCommentAt i children =
    if i < 0 then
        False

    else
        Array.get i children |> Maybe.map nodeIsComment |> Maybe.withDefault False


{-| How many comments `nodes` starts with. -}
commentPrefixLength : Array LPNode -> Int
commentPrefixLength nodes =
    (Array.foldl
        (\n acc ->
            if acc.stopped || not (nodeIsComment n) then
                { acc | stopped = True }

            else
                { acc | count = acc.count + 1 }
        )
        { count = 0, stopped = False }
        nodes
    ).count


{-| The finding, if any, for the comment run in one gap — `Nothing` when the run
is not one this audit makes a claim about, or when either assembly has no render
truth to offer. -}
runFinding : LPNode -> Bool -> Array LPNode -> { start : Int, end : Int } -> Maybe Finding
runFinding node liesBelow children run =
    let
        members =
            Array.slice run.start run.end children

        -- How many real items precede this run: also its predecessor's ordinal
        -- among the items, and its successor's, in EITHER assembly. Removing
        -- comments does not renumber them.
        itemsBefore =
            Array.slice 0 run.start children
                |> Array.keepIf (\n -> not (nodeIsComment n))
                |> Array.length

        itemsAfter =
            Array.dropFirst run.end children
                |> Array.keepIf (\n -> not (nodeIsComment n))
                |> Array.length

        without =
            Array.slice 0 run.start children ++ Array.dropFirst run.end children
    in
    if itemsBefore < 1 || itemsAfter < 1 then
        Nothing

    else
        when
            { withIt = itemGap itemsBefore children
            , withoutIt = itemGap itemsBefore without
            }
        is
            { withIt = Just gapWith, withoutIt = Just gapWithout } ->
                let
                    -- The predicate's own fold, restricted to this gap: the run
                    -- plus the one real item that follows it, which is all
                    -- `commentBreaksFlowRow` needs to answer for this gap and is
                    -- the only way to ask it rather than re-derive it.
                    claim =
                        commentBreaksFlowRow (Array.slice run.start (run.end + 1) children)
                in
                if gapWithout > 0 || claim == (gapWith > gapWithout) then
                    Nothing

                else
                    Just
                        { predicate = "commentBreaksFlowRow"
                        , boxKind =
                            shapeKindName (lpnShape node)
                                ++ " / "
                                ++ String.join "+" (Array.map (\m -> shapeKindName (lpnShape m)) members)
                        , row = Array.first members |> Maybe.andThen lpnFirstPos |> Maybe.map (\p -> p.row) |> Maybe.withDefault -1
                        , col = Array.first members |> Maybe.andThen lpnFirstPos |> Maybe.map (\p -> p.col) |> Maybe.withDefault -1
                        , rendered = renderedFlow children
                        , propagated = liesBelow
                        , claim = claim
                        }

            _ ->
                Nothing


{-| Assemble `nodes` as a gluing flow and return how many rows separate item
`k - 1`'s last row from item `k`'s first — counting only the non-comment items,
so `k` names the same pair whether or not a comment sits between them. `Nothing`
if the flow will not render or has fewer than `k + 1` items. -}
itemGap : Int -> Array LPNode -> Maybe Int
itemGap k nodes =
    when buildFlowBoxMeasured 0 nodes is
        Err _ ->
            Nothing

        Ok assembled ->
            let
                items =
                    placeRows assembled.measures
                        |> Array.keepIf (\p -> not (nodeIsComment p.node))
            in
            when { prev = Array.get (k - 1) items, next = Array.get k items } is
                { prev = Just prev, next = Just next } ->
                    Just (next.firstRow - prev.lastRow)

                _ ->
                    Nothing


renderedFlow : Array LPNode -> String
renderedFlow nodes =
    when buildFlowBoxMeasured 0 nodes is
        Ok assembled ->
            String.trim (B.render assembled.box)

        Err e ->
            e


{-| Which shape kinds hand their children straight to the gluing flow assembler
(`buildFlowBox 0 children` / the `assembleFlow` arm of `makePBox`). A kind that
routes its children somewhere else -- a bracket container, a binop chain, a
`when` -- is not what this predicate is asked about.
-}
dispatchesToGluingFlow : LPShape -> Bool
dispatchesToGluingFlow shape =
    when shape is
        AcrossOrVertical _ ->
            True

        BodyBlock ->
            True

        IndentedBlock ->
            True

        SoftIndentedBlock ->
            True

        _ ->
            False


{-| Each placed item with the rows it occupies in the assembled box. Row
arithmetic as `FlowAssembly.typeContentSpansRows` does it: running the row
additions up gives each item's last row, and its own height gives back its first.
-}
placeRows : Array FlowMeasure -> Array { node : LPNode, firstRow : Int, lastRow : Int }
placeRows measures =
    (Array.foldl
        (\m acc ->
            let
                endRow =
                    acc.total + m.rowsAdded - 1
            in
            { total = acc.total + m.rowsAdded
            , out =
                Array.pushLast
                    { node = m.node
                    , firstRow = endRow - (B.height m.box - 1)
                    , lastRow = endRow
                    }
                    acc.out
            }
        )
        { total = 0, out = [] }
        measures
    ).out


checkNode : LPNode -> Bool -> Array Finding
checkNode node liesBelow =
    flowCommentFindings node liesBelow ++ checkShapePredicates node liesBelow


checkShapePredicates : LPNode -> Bool -> Array Finding
checkShapePredicates node liesBelow =
    when makePBox (lpnShape node) (lpnChildren node) is
        -- A node that cannot render on its own (RootBox, OriginalRows, or a
        -- construct whose builder needs context its parent holds) yields no
        -- render truth to compare against, so it proves nothing either way.
        Err _ ->
            []

        Ok box ->
            if isSingleLine box then
                auditedPredicates
                    |> Array.keepIf (\p -> p.test node)
                    |> Array.map (\p -> makeFinding p.name node box liesBelow)

            else
                []


makeFinding : String -> LPNode -> Box -> Bool -> Finding
makeFinding name node box liesBelow =
    { predicate = name
    , boxKind = shapeKindName (lpnShape node)
    , row = lpnFirstPos node |> Maybe.map (\p -> p.row) |> Maybe.withDefault -1
    , col = lpnFirstPos node |> Maybe.map (\p -> p.col) |> Maybe.withDefault -1
    , rendered = String.trim (B.render box)
    , propagated = liesBelow
    , claim = True
    }


{-| The constructor name, plus the detail that decides the layout (a bracket
kind, or whether the author already forced it vertical) -- enough to tell one
finding's shape from another's without going back to `--lpt`.
-}
shapeKindName : LPShape -> String
shapeKindName shape =
    let
        forced fv =
            if fv then
                " forceVertical"

            else
                ""
    in
    when shape is
        RootBox ->
            "RootBox"

        OriginalRows r ->
            "OriginalRows " ++ stypeToString r.stype

        EmptyLine ->
            "EmptyLine"

        UnbreakableText _ ->
            "UnbreakableText"

        SynthesizedText _ ->
            "SynthesizedText"

        SingleLineComment _ ->
            "SingleLineComment"

        BlockComment _ ->
            "BlockComment"

        DocComment _ ->
            "DocComment"

        AcrossOrVertical r ->
            "AcrossOrVertical" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        IfCondition r ->
            "IfCondition" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        AllAcrossOrAllVertical _ ->
            "AllAcrossOrAllVertical"

        AlwaysVertical _ ->
            "AlwaysVertical"

        RecordUpdate r ->
            "RecordUpdate" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        EmptyBracketed _ ->
            "EmptyBracketed"

        IndentedBlock ->
            "IndentedBlock"

        PipelineStep ->
            "PipelineStep"

        Pipeline r ->
            "Pipeline" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        BodyBlock ->
            "BodyBlock"

        WhenBranch ->
            "WhenBranch"

        WhenBranchPattern ->
            "WhenBranchPattern"

        ParenBlock r ->
            "ParenBlock" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        OpAndRhs ->
            "OpAndRhs"

        Binop r ->
            "Binop" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        WhenFlow r ->
            "WhenFlow" ++ forced r.forceVertical

        PrefixGlue _ ->
            "PrefixGlue"

        Glue ->
            "Glue"

        SoftIndentedBlock ->
            "SoftIndentedBlock"

        MultilineString _ ->
            "MultilineString"


{-| Encode findings for the `--audit-predicates` flag. -}
findingsToJson : Array Finding -> String
findingsToJson findings =
    findings
        |> Array.map
            (\f ->
                Json.object
                    [ { key = "predicate", value = Json.string f.predicate }
                    , { key = "boxKind", value = Json.string f.boxKind }
                    , { key = "row", value = Json.int f.row }
                    , { key = "col", value = Json.int f.col }
                    , { key = "rendered", value = Json.string f.rendered }
                    , { key = "propagated", value = Json.bool f.propagated }
                    , { key = "claim", value = Json.bool f.claim }
                    ]
            )
        |> Json.array identity
        |> Json.encode 2
